```python
class Logger(Protocol):
    def log(self, message: str) -> None: ...

class XmlLogger:  # legacy, can't change
    def write_xml_entry(self, payload: str, severity: int) -> None:
        ...

class XmlLoggerAdapter:
    def __init__(self, legacy: XmlLogger):
        self._legacy = legacy

    def log(self, message: str) -> None:
        self._legacy.write_xml_entry(payload=message, severity=1)
```

Any code expecting a `Logger` can now take an `XmlLoggerAdapter(XmlLogger())` and call `.log(...)` normally — the legacy class never had to change, and the adapter is the only place that knows `write_xml_entry` exists.
